<!--
  ComboboxMultiDemo.rozie — content-STABLE chip-rail screenshot fixture for the
  pure-Rozie Combobox `multiple` mode (Phase 86 R1, plan 86-05).

  The one thing no happy-dom mount-and-drive behavioral test
  (multiple.behavior.test.ts) can prove: the chip rail's real on-screen
  appearance — its wrapping, its spacing inside the control, and the popup
  width matching the WHOLE control (chips + input), not the input alone. This
  demo exists ONLY to make that a deterministic pixel cell — never extend it
  for behavior (mirrors ComboboxScreenshotDemo.rozie / ComboboxFloatingDemo.
  rozie's own "never bolt behavior onto the screenshot cell" discipline).

  Made deterministic by:
    1. `multiple` set with a FIXED three-value seeded selection
       (apple/banana/cherry) bound `r-model:value="$data.value"` against a
       five-option list, so three chips render (at rest, chip order === the
       seeded array order) and two options (Date, Elderberry) remain
       unselected in the popup — giving a single frame that shows both the
       chip rail's steady state AND the popup's selected/unselected marking
       together.
    2. The popup is opened DETERMINISTICALLY at mount via the composed
       Combobox's own `focus` handle — `$refs.multiCombobox.focus()`,
       deferred one macrotask (setTimeout(0)) past `$onMount`, mirroring
       ComboboxFloatingDemo.rozie's identical child-ref-readiness workaround
       (a child's $expose handle is not yet registered when a PARENT's
       $onMount runs synchronously on Solid — a synchronous call there
       no-ops and the popup never opens). Opening the popup in the same
       frame is what makes the width-match-spans-chips-plus-input truth
       (D-13) a visible, not just asserted, fact.
    3. An explicit `height` on the root box (not auto-sized from flow
       content) — mirrors ComboboxFloatingDemo.rozie's point 2: the popup's
       `position: absolute` panel does not grow an ancestor's auto height,
       so without an explicit height tall enough for chips + input + the
       five-option open popup, the popup would render OUTSIDE
       `[data-testid="rozie-mount"]`'s captured bounding box and never be
       captured.
    4. A DISTINCT idBase so the aria ids never collide with any other
       combobox cell.

  One .rozie source compiles to six per-framework screenshot consumers; the
  Linux PNG baseline is generated separately in pinned Linux Docker (NOT here
  — feedback_vr_linux_baselines). Until it lands the matrix cell auto-fixmes
  on `baselineExists()`.
-->

<rozie name="ComboboxMultiDemo">

<components>
{
  Combobox: '../../packages/ui/combobox/src/Combobox.rozie',
}
</components>

<data>
{
  value: ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry'],
}
</data>

<script>
const OPTIONS = [
  { value: 'apple', label: 'Apple' },
  { value: 'banana', label: 'Banana' },
  { value: 'cherry', label: 'Cherry' },
  { value: 'date', label: 'Date' },
  { value: 'elderberry', label: 'Elderberry' },
]

// See the header comment (point 2) for why this is deferred one macrotask
// past $onMount rather than called synchronously.
$onMount(() => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    $refs.multiCombobox.focus()
  }, 0)
})
</script>

<template>
<div class="combobox-multi-demo">
  <Combobox
    ref="multiCombobox"
    r-model:value="$data.value"
    :options="OPTIONS"
    multiple
    placeholder="Add more…"
    ariaLabel="Fruit (multi-select)"
    idBase="multi-combobox"
  />
</div>
</template>

<style>
.combobox-multi-demo {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
  color: #1a1a1a;
  width: 360px;
  height: 380px;
  padding: 1rem;
}
</style>

</rozie>
